            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Core Conclusion: AI Copy Homogenization Stems from Usage, Not Tools</h2>
<p>When most marketers use identical prompt templates to generate ad copy, AI amplifies "mediocrity" rather than "efficiency." The real issue: we're treating AI as an "auto-generator" instead of a "creative assistant."</p>
<p>Take this example from a utility app's overseas advertising team testing Meta and TikTok campaigns:<br />
- <strong>Traditional approach</strong>: Ask ChatGPT directly for "Get the best [product] now!" style copy → CTR consistently 15% below industry average<br />
- <strong>Optimized approach</strong>: Human operators first define differentiated angles, then AI assists expansion into multiple versions → A/B testing shows 22% CTR improvement</p>
<p>The difference isn't the AI itself—it's whether humans complete the "differentiation decision" step first.</p>
<h2>Why Your AI Copy Always Lacks "Personality"</h2>
<p>AI excels at imitation and recombination but struggles to create genuine differentiation from scratch. Here are three common pitfalls:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Myth 1</strong>: Give AI only a product name and let it run free → Result: blend into the crowd<br />
<strong>Myth 2</strong>: Use generic prompt templates for batch generation → Multiple accounts produce highly overlapping creatives<br />
<strong>Myth 3</strong>: Over-rely on AI drafts without human intervention → Missing brand tone and localized warmth</p>
</blockquote>
<p>One cross-border e-commerce team used the same prompt template to generate 20 ad variants for Facebook. After launch, they discovered 13 variants had over 70% copy similarity, causing the system to flag them as duplicate content and cut impressions in half.</p>
<h2>3 Steps to Genuinely Differentiated AI Copy</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Lock in the "Differentiation Angle" BEFORE Using AI</h3>
<p>Don't let AI decide "what to say"—humans must decide "from which angle to say it."</p>
<p>For example, with a sunscreen product:<br />
- Generic angle: SPF50+, long-lasting protection<br />
- Differentiated angle: "While other moms reapply sunscreen, you're taking photos" (scene-based pain point)</p>
<p>The former can be written by AI in countless versions; the latter represents the operator's core competitive advantage.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Give AI a "Persona" to Reduce Assembly-Line Output</h3>
<p>Include in your prompts:<br />
- Target audience profile (not just "women 25-35," but "first-time moms taking kids to tropical islands")<br />
- Common competitor copy tones ("Avoid cliché phrases like 'best ever'")<br />
- Brand personality (humorous/professional/warm/cool)</p>
<p>This provides AI with a creative framework rather than an open-ended task.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Build a "Human Filter → AI Expand" Workflow</h3>
<p>Recommended process:<br />
1. Operators generate 3-5 core angle options (30 minutes)<br />
2. AI expands into 10-15 copy variants (5 minutes)<br />
3. Human curation keeps 5-8 genuinely differentiated versions (20 minutes)<br />
4. Launch and iterate based on performance data</p>
<p>This workflow adds 40 minutes versus "pure AI generation → direct launch," but CTR improvements typically exceed 20%.</p>
<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The antidote to AI copy homogenization isn't finding a better AI tool—it's marketers reclaiming "decision-making authority": let AI handle execution efficiency, humans handle differentiation judgment. Work together, don't let AI run naked.</p>
